{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2094082456/3/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a2d730bd\" lines=\"43-56\">\n<description>\nExtend each solid foreground rectangle toward its same-color isolated pixels with capped one-cell paths, clearing each original marker pixel.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each foreground color has exactly one solid rectangular object and one or more isolated same-color marker pixels.\n- Every marker is horizontally or vertically aligned with the rectangle bbox and lies outside it.\nRule\n- For each marker, draw a one-cell-wide straight path from the adjacent side of the rectangle through one cell beyond the marker.\n- Add a three-pixel perpendicular cap at the rectangle end and around the marker endpoint.\n- Recolor the original marker pixel to background.\nKey insight\n- The isolated pixels are not copied; they mark the hollow endpoints of capped protrusions grown from their matching rectangle.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5623160b\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nPush every colored object touching maroon markers straight outward until it reaches the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Maroon pixels are fixed markers.\n- Every other foreground 4-connected object touches the marker pixels on exactly one cardinal side.\n\nRule:\n- Clear each non-background, non-maroon object from its original position.\n- Translate it in the direction away from its maroon contact until the object reaches the grid boundary.\n- Keep the maroon marker pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The maroon contact side is a launcher: it determines a unique outward ray for the whole object, and the edge of the grid determines the translation distance.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e84fef15\" lines=\"39-59\">\n<description>\nReconstruct the repeated tile from its separated copies and mark the missing pixels in the damaged copy blue.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a lattice of equal-size blocks separated by one-cell solid rows\n  and columns of the same separator color.\n- All blocks are copies of one motif except exactly one block, where some\n  motif pixels have been overwritten by the motif background color.\n- Blue is absent from the input and is reserved as the output marker color.\n\nRule:\n- Recover the motif by taking the unique majority color at each local block\n  coordinate across all block copies.\n- Find the single block that differs from this consensus motif.\n- Return the consensus motif, recoloring the differing local coordinates blue.\n\nKey insight:\n- The repeated copies provide redundancy: the damaged positions are exactly\n  the coordinates where the lone nonconforming copy has background instead of\n  the consensus motif color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8fff9e47\" lines=\"8-25\">\n<description>\nTurn a 4-cell-wide strip of 2x2 blocks into four nested corner-fill patterns.\n\nInput structure\nThe input is an even-sized rectangle whose shorter dimension is four pixels.\nIt can be partitioned exactly into 2x2 blocks, giving one block for each layer\nof the output quadrants.\n\nRule\nRead the 2x2 blocks in reverse row-major order: bottom-right block first,\nmoving left, then moving upward. For each block, send its top-left,\ntop-right, bottom-left, and bottom-right pixels to the layer sequences of the\nupper-left, upper-right, lower-left, and lower-right output quadrants.\n\nKey insight\nEach input 2x2 block supplies the four colors for one concentric layer, and\nthe layer order is the block order from bottom-right back to top-left.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f5f939b\" lines=\"42-57\">\n<description>\nMark the missing center of each plus-shaped arrangement of four two-pixel bars with yellow.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The single foreground color forms isolated 4-connected objects, each a horizontal or vertical two-pixel bar.\n- Some bars form an implied plus sign: two horizontal bars and two vertical bars around an empty center, with a one-pixel gap between the center and each bar.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input unchanged except at implied plus-sign centers.\n- For every background pixel with foreground bars at offsets 2-3 cells left, right, above, and below, recolor that center yellow.\n- The one-pixel gaps immediately next to the center in the four cardinal directions must remain background.\n\nKey insight:\n- The yellow pixels are not extending a line; they identify the shared missing center where four separated two-pixel bars align as a plus sign.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1478ab18\" lines=\"35-43\">\n<description>\nPaint the teal outline of the right triangle determined by the missing corner of the grey-marker square.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is an orange background with exactly four foreground marker pixels of one color. The markers have a square bounding box: three markers are bbox corners, and the fourth marker is inside that square.\nRule\nFind the one corner of the marker bounding square that has no marker. That missing corner is the right-angle vertex of a right isosceles triangle; paint its two axis-aligned legs and the opposite diagonal in teal, leaving existing marker pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\nThe present corner marker opposite the missing corner is a decoy; the three triangle vertices are the missing corner and the two marker corners adjacent to it.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"71e489b6\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nHighlight black defects in blue foreground by surrounding them with orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- Blue (1) foreground on black (0) bg; small black holes or protrusions within blue regions; occasional thin blue spurs into black areas.\n\nRule:\n- Thin blue pixels mostly surrounded by black are absorbed into the background.\n- Small black components (≤3 cells) and leaf pixels of larger black components are \"defects.\"\n- Each defect gets an orange (7) 8-neighbor halo; the defect pixels themselves stay black.\n\nKey insight: Only structural anomalies—tiny isolated patches and dangling endpoints—are marked, not bulk black regions.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f3e14006\" lines=\"21-39\">\n<description>\nBuild the woven rectangle determined by the two marked cells on each crossed bar.\n\nInput structure\nA black-background grid contains one long horizontal bar and one long vertical bar.\nEach bar has a dominant base color and exactly two same-colored marker pixels.\n\nRule\nThe horizontal marker pair gives the output column span.  From the horizontal bar\nrow, walk toward the farther vertical marker; rows and columns alternate every\nother cell.  Even step/even offset cells use the horizontal marker color until\nthe nearer vertical marker is reached, then use the vertical marker color.\nOdd row steps use the horizontal base on marker columns and the original\ncrossing color on spacer columns.  Spacer columns on even row steps use the\nvertical base, except at the switch row where they use the crossing color.\n\nKey insight\nThe original cross cell records which base color wins at base/base lattice\ncrossings; the nearer vertical marker is the color-switch boundary.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"37ce87bb\" lines=\"33-47\">\n<description>\nCompute a grey result bar by adding teal bar heights and subtracting red bar heights.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background and several bottom-aligned one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Teal bars are positive operands; red bars are negative operands.\n- The penultimate column is an empty result slot.\n\nRule\n- Keep every input bar unchanged.\n- Compute result height = total teal bar height - total red bar height.\n- Paint that many bottom cells of the penultimate column grey.\n\nKey insight\n- The bars encode arithmetic by height, with color providing the sign.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8e301a54\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nMove every foreground object straight downward by a number of rows equal to its own pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- The foreground is made of separated 4-connected monochromatic objects.\n- Objects may move partly below the bottom edge of the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the canvas to the background.\n- For each foreground object, compute its area in pixels.\n- Redraw the object at the same columns and that many rows lower, clipping pixels below the grid.\n\nKey insight:\n- The downward displacement is the object's own area; color and shape only identify which pixels move together.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"11dc524f\" lines=\"25-40\">\n<description>\nSlide the non-square sprite to the square anchor and replace the anchor with the sprite's mirror image.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- There is one 8-connected non-background sprite that is not a solid 2x2 square.\n- There is one solid 2x2 square anchor in another non-background color.\n\nRule:\n- Move the sprite straight toward the anchor along their unique cardinal separation until it touches the anchor's near edge.\n- Reflect the moved sprite across that near edge and draw the reflected copy in the anchor color.\n- Clear the original locations; the output keeps only the moved sprite and reflected anchor-color copy.\n\nKey insight:\n- The 2x2 square marks the mirror line: its side facing the sprite becomes the contact edge for a two-color reflected pair.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"52df9849\" lines=\"49-67\">\n<description>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
